PARITY SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  The web SignalSelector is a thin controlled wrapper over `ComboboxMulti`:

    [ SIGNALS (value.length / max)  (?) ]      ← uppercase muted label + HelpTooltip(help.signal.layers)
    [ 🔍  Search signals…              ▾ ]      ← ComboboxMulti, mono options, maxItems cap, slice(0,cap)

  Props: options:string[] · value:string[] · onChange(next) · max=5|null · showLayerHelp=true ·
  labelOverride. The wrapper's whole job is: the "Signals (N / max)" (or "(N)", or override) label, the
  optional layer-help tooltip (i18nKey "help.signal.layers" + ariaLabel "help.signal.layers.aria"), the
  search-icon + "Search signals…" placeholder, mono signal-name options, and the hard cap —
  `onChange={(next) => onChange(Number.isFinite(cap) ? next.slice(0, cap) : next)}` (cap defaults to 5,
  `max=null` ⇒ uncapped). Only hook: `useTranslation`.

  Native port reproduces all of it:
   • Shared counterparts (prompt "must map to native counterparts"): web `ComboboxMulti`→`TSComboboxMulti`
     (@/components/forms → Sources/Components/Forms/Inputs.swift); web `HelpTooltip`→`TSHelpTooltip`
     (@/components/ui → Sources/Components/UI/Overlays.swift). Both consumed as-is, not re-implemented.
   • The "Signals (N / max)" / "(N)" / override label, the layer-help tooltip with the web ariaLabel
     override, the placeholder, and the cap — the cap is enforced through the bound model
     (`SignalSelectorProjection.reconcile` → `applyCap`, the exact analogue of `next.slice(0, cap)`),
     because `TSComboboxMulti` owns its own option rendering + search chrome and exposes only a
     `Set`-valued binding. The wrapper maps that `Set`-edit back to an ordered, capped selection.
   • Data binds through `SignalSelectorModel` (P1/S8 seam mirroring LoadableState / LiveConnectionState);
     the view performs no HTTP. The web `options` (upstream `useSignals` / `/signals/{id}/available`) are
     the bound candidate list. P1/S11 `view.opened` telemetry fires once on appear with slug
     "SignalSelector".

  HIG adaptation (covenant-clean, documented): the web caps via `ComboboxMulti`'s internal `maxItems` and
  renders each option with a custom mono `renderOption`; the native shared `TSComboboxMulti` owns those
  internals and does not expose a `maxItems`/`renderOption` seam, and modifying that atomic component is
  OUT OF SCOPE for this prompt (allowed files = SignalSelector.* + this log; atomic shared components are
  the P4 component-library bundle's responsibility). The wrapper therefore enforces the identical cap
  semantics through the model (web `slice(0, cap)`) and passes the signal name verbatim as the option
  title. Composition (label + tooltip + capped multi-select) and every web i18n key are preserved.

